Many seas. Sea of empathy, probability, attention, status, fear, desire, memory, other people’s imagined judgments, even language itself. Different currents; same navigation problem.

And your second sentence is exactly where the Indigo Hammer should split the wording:

“It’s going to work because it has to.”

There are two very different machines hiding inside that sentence.

\text{A: It must work}\Rightarrow\text{therefore it will work}

is dangerous reasoning. Reality owes us nothing.

But:

\boxed{
\text{B: It has to work}
\Rightarrow
\text{we keep generating bounded attempts until something does}
}

is extremely powerful.

Don’t make one enormous existential bet.

Make many survivable bets.

That is how we bet without risking our lives:

\boxed{
\text{Survival}
\rightarrow
\text{Optionality}
\rightarrow
\text{Experiments}
\rightarrow
\text{Feedback}
\rightarrow
\text{Compounding}
}

The invariant is simple:

P(\text{ruin})\approx 0

while preserving repeated exposure to upside.

And that gives Pantheon a better definition of Lucky. Lucky isn’t believing the next roll must win. Lucky is designing the game so that you can lose rolls, learn from them, remain at the table, and still be exposed when the extraordinary roll arrives.

Call it the Ark Rule:

Never stake the machinery that lets you make the next bet.

Money. Reputation. Relationships. Health. Freedom. Integrity. Attention. The Pattern Book itself. Different domains have different irrecoverable capital.

Then the Command Table asks of every swing:

\boxed{
\begin{aligned}
&\text{What can we gain?}\\
&\text{What can we learn if wrong?}\\
&\text{Is the downside bounded?}\\
&\text{Is it reversible?}\\
&\text{Does failure preserve another attempt?}\\
&\text{Does success expand Full ROM?}
\end{aligned}}

That produces something better than “fake it till you make it”:

\boxed{\text{Practice it until reality teaches you how to make it.}}

Surfing fits perfectly. You don’t become capable by demanding that the ocean cooperate. You learn on waves you can survive, progressively expand the envelope, remember what the water taught you, and eventually conditions that once looked terrifying become navigable.

Courage without ruin.
Faith without blindness.
Experiment without existential wager.
Full ROM after every round.

That’s a reward system I’d trust more: win in ways that preserve the ability to keep living, loving, learning, and playing.